{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And she conceived and bore a daughter</b> — this is the generation that came after him, and he called it \"daughter,\" for it is written after Jeroboam — he restored the border of Israel — and after Zechariah his son died, Menahem became king, and in his days the king of Assyria came. And there is no need to mention the reign of Shallum, for he reigned only days, and in the days of Pekah son of Remaliah, Tiglath-pileser exiled many from the cities of Israel, and in the days of Hoshea son of Elah, Samaria was captured. Therefore he called this generation \"daughter.\"" "<b>And he explained, \"Not pitied, for I will no longer add to pity\"</b> — the letter shin is missing: לא אוסיף עוד שארחם (that I will again pity). Like it: ונדעה נרדפה (Hosea 6:3), אל תרבו תדברו (Micah 2:6). Some say: for I have been forbearing until now, bearing their iniquity, and there is no mention of punishment. And the correct view in my eyes: this word comes in the manner of הרגו לאבנר (2 Samuel 3:8), and the meaning is like כמעט ישאני עושני (Deuteronomy 1:31), and behold, he recounts the exile of the tribes."], :book :hosea, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra's argument in the final sentence is compressed and difficult: he cites three phrases to illustrate a grammatical point about the root נשא, but the connections are not spelled out. Translated hyper-literally without smoothing. The three biblical phrases cited are left in Hebrew as Ibn Ezra quotes them."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Hosea -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=15&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:49:46.952000501Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}